CAT = "s7Trading"; SEG = "TRD"
Q = [
("A customer owns stock at $52 and wants to protect a gain if the price falls below $48. The order to enter is a:",
 "Sell stop at 48", ["Sell limit at 48", "Buy stop at 48", "Buy limit at 48"],
 "A sell stop sits below the market and becomes a market order when the stock trades at or below 48. A sell limit at 48 below the market would execute right away."),
("A customer is short a stock at $30 and wants to limit losses if it rises. The order to enter is a:",
 "Buy stop above the market", ["Buy limit below the market", "Sell stop below the market", "Sell limit above the market"],
 "A buy stop above the market triggers a purchase to cover the short if the price rises to the stop. Buy limits sit below the market."),
("A customer enters a sell stop-limit order at 40 stop, 39.75 limit. After a trade at 40, the order:",
 "Becomes a limit order at 39.75", ["Becomes a market order to sell", "Is cancelled automatically", "Executes only at exactly 40"],
 "When the stop price is reached, a stop-limit order becomes a limit order, here to sell at 39.75 or better. It may not fill if the price falls through the limit."),
("A customer buys stock regular way on a Friday. Assuming no holidays, the trade settles on:",
 "Monday", ["Friday", "Tuesday", "Wednesday"],
 "Regular-way settlement is T+1, one business day after trade date, so a Friday trade settles the following Monday."),
("A customer buys $20,000 of stock in a new margin account. Under Regulation T, the required deposit is:",
 "$10,000", ["$5,000", "$2,000", "$20,000"],
 "Reg T initial margin is 50% of the purchase, here $10,000. FINRA also requires a $2,000 minimum equity, which this deposit already meets."),
("A customer buys $3,000 of stock in a new margin account. The minimum deposit required is:",
 "$2,000", ["$1,500", "$3,000", "$1,000"],
 "Reg T would require only $1,500, but FINRA's minimum equity of $2,000 applies. If the purchase were under $2,000, the customer would pay in full."),
("A long margin account has a market value of $40,000 and a debit of $24,000. The equity is:",
 "$16,000", ["$24,000", "$64,000", "$20,000"],
 "Equity in a long account = market value - debit balance = $40,000 - $24,000 = $16,000."),
("A long margin account has a debit balance of $15,000. At what market value would it hit FINRA's 25% minimum maintenance?",
 "$20,000", ["$18,750", "$30,000", "$60,000"],
 "The maintenance point = debit / 0.75 = $15,000 / 0.75 = $20,000. At that value equity is $5,000, exactly 25%."),
("A customer sells short $30,000 of stock in a margin account. FINRA's minimum maintenance on the short position is:",
 "30% of market value", ["25% of market value", "50% of market value", "100% of market value"],
 "Short positions in stocks priced $5 or more need maintenance equity of 30% of market value. Long positions need 25%."),
("A short margin account has a credit balance of $26,000. At what market value would it hit the 30% maintenance level?",
 "$20,000", ["$18,200", "$33,800", "$26,000"],
 "For short accounts, the maintenance point = credit balance / 1.30 = $26,000 / 1.3 = $20,000. Equity is then $6,000, 30% of $20,000."),
("A long margin account has a market value of $50,000 and a debit of $20,000. With Reg T at 50%, the excess equity is:",
 "$5,000", ["$30,000", "$10,000", "$25,000"],
 "Equity = $50,000 - $20,000 = $30,000. Reg T requirement = 50% of $50,000 = $25,000. Excess equity, which credits SMA, = $5,000."),
("Special memorandum account (SMA) balances can be used to:",
 "Buy securities or withdraw cash", ["Lower the Reg T requirement", "Meet a maintenance call in full", "Raise the maintenance level"],
 "SMA is a line of credit created by excess equity. It can fund purchases at twice its value or be withdrawn as cash, if maintenance is still met."),
("Before accepting a short sale order in an equity, Regulation SHO requires the firm to:",
 "Locate shares that can be borrowed", ["Wait for an uptick in the price", "Get FINRA approval for the trade", "Deliver shares on trade date"],
 "Reg SHO's locate requirement means the firm must have reasonable grounds to believe the shares can be borrowed by settlement. The old uptick rule was repealed; a price test applies only after a 10% drop."),
("Under Regulation SHO, after a stock drops 10% from the prior close in a day, short sales:",
 "Must be priced above the best bid", ["Are banned for the rest of the year", "Must be executed only at the close", "Need a principal's written approval"],
 "The alternative uptick rule (Rule 201) restricts short sales to prices above the current national best bid for the rest of that day and the next."),
("A customer buys a corporate bond regular way on a Thursday. Accrued interest runs up to but not including:",
 "Friday, settlement date", ["Thursday, trade date", "Monday", "The next coupon date"],
 "The buyer pays accrued interest from the last coupon date up to, but not including, the settlement date. With T+1, a Thursday trade settles Friday."),
("Which item is NOT required on a customer trade confirmation for a stock purchase?",
 "The rep's past commissions", ["The trade date", "Whether the firm acted as agent or principal", "The price per share"],
 "Confirmations show trade date, security, price, quantity, capacity (agent or principal) and commission or markup details. A history of the rep's commissions is not part of it."),
("A customer's account statement must be sent at least:",
 "Quarterly", ["Monthly", "Annually", "Only after trades"],
 "FINRA Rule 2231 requires statements at least once each calendar quarter when the account has a balance or activity. Many firms send them monthly when there is activity."),
("An affiliate of a reporting company wants to sell control stock under Rule 144. The volume limit in any 3 months is the greater of 1% of outstanding shares or:",
 "The average weekly volume of four weeks", ["The average daily volume over one month", "The total trading volume of the prior week", "5% of the trailing year's volume"],
 "Rule 144 limits sales to the greater of 1% of outstanding shares or the average weekly trading volume over the four calendar weeks before filing."),
("Restricted stock of a company that has been reporting for over 90 days may be sold under Rule 144 after a holding period of:",
 "Six months", ["One year", "Two years", "Ninety days"],
 "For SEC reporting companies the holding period is six months from full payment; for non-reporting companies it is one year."),
("A customer complains in writing about an unauthorized trade. The firm must:",
 "Keep a record of the complaint", ["Reverse the trade without review", "Send it to the SEC within a day", "Destroy it once settled"],
 "FINRA Rules 4513 and 4530 require firms to keep written complaints and record their handling, and to report statistics to FINRA."),
("A firm sells a stock it holds in inventory to a customer and adds a markup. The firm acted as a:",
 "Principal", ["Agent", "Broker", "Transfer agent"],
 "A dealer acting as principal sells from its own account and earns a markup. A broker acting as agent matches buyer and seller for a commission."),
("Under FINRA's 5% policy, a markup is judged mainly from:",
 "The prevailing market price", ["The firm's original cost", "The customer's account size", "The prior day's close"],
 "Markups are measured from the prevailing market (usually the inside offer for buys), not the firm's cost. Five percent is a guideline, not a hard limit."),
("A customer's order is placed with no instruction on timing. It is treated as:",
 "A day order", ["Good till cancelled", "Fill or kill", "Immediate or cancel"],
 "Unless the customer specifies otherwise, an order expires at the end of the trading day. GTC orders stay open until filled or cancelled."),
("A company pays a 5% stock dividend. A customer's open buy limit order on the stock will be:",
 "Adjusted on the ex-date", ["Cancelled automatically", "Left unchanged", "Converted to a market order"],
 "On the ex-date, open buy limit and sell stop orders are reduced for cash and stock dividends, and the share count is raised for stock distributions, unless marked do not reduce."),
]
